# Domain Building Blocks Conventions

This governs how each tactical DDD piece in the domain layer is written: value objects, identifiers, literal sets, errors, entities, parts and events, plus the mapper that rebuilds an entity from a row. Every piece is built from one base factory or type in the shared kernel, so two entities or two value objects never differ in shape, only in content. Where these pieces live (folders, file kinds, the barrel) is the layering rule's concern, not this one's. Where it needs a language's own mechanism (file extension, visibility, how a type is made immutable or distinct, how an interface is marked), the language idioms rule for the file's extension supplies it.

The root of a sub-slice is its entity (what DDD calls the aggregate root), and the other entities it owns are its parts. The word aggregate names neither a piece nor a file kind here.

---

## Cohesion and essential complexity

- Keep code that changes together in one place. A rule lives with the entity or value object it constrains, not in a helper far away, because a rule scattered across files makes every change to it touch all of them.
- Accept essential complexity. A large entity or policy that mirrors a genuinely complex domain is correct, and size alone is not a smell. Splitting, abstracting or generalizing it to look smaller adds indirection that means nothing in the domain and hides the rules a reader came to find.
- Avoid accidental complexity. Anything the domain doesn't demand (a hand-rolled variant of a shared base, a wrapper, an extra layer of indirection, a duplicated shape, mapping glue) is removed. The test is whether a domain rule can be named that requires it; if none can, it is accidental.

## One formula per piece

| Piece | Built from | Shape it always has |
|---|---|---|
| Value object | The shared kernel's distinct-primitive support plus its text factory (or a hand-written parser for a non-text value) | A same-named declaration with `kind`, `parse` and `is`, and a distinct type derived from it |
| Identifier | The shared kernel's identifier factory, one declaration per id kind | Same as a value object; ids are lowercase UUIDs |
| Literal set | The shared kernel's literal-set factory | A declaration with `kind`, `values`, `parse` and `is`, and a type derived from its values |
| Error | The base domain error's `define` factory, one error type per sub-slice | A closed set of `snake_case` codes with fixed messages |
| Entity | The shared kernel's entity base, typed by a state type that extends the base entity state; the root of the sub-slice | Frozen instance, `restore`, `sameAs`, protected `with`, plus intention-named creation factories |
| Part | The same entity base: an entity owned by the root | Same as the entity, created and changed only through the root |
| Event | The shared kernel's domain event type | `type`, `entityId`, `occurredAt`, `payload` |

A piece that seems to need a shape outside this table is a signal to extend the shared-kernel base once, for every context, not to hand-roll a variant in one sub-slice. A hand-rolled variant is how two entities end up with two different ways to rebuild from storage.

Operation and field names (`restore`, `sameAs`, `with`, `entityId`, `occurredAt`) are written in the language's case, and the language idioms rule says how a reserved word is avoided.

## Value objects, identifiers and literal sets

- A value object is a primitive carrying its own type, with a same-named declaration holding its parser. The parser is its only constructor: it normalizes, validates and fails with the sub-slice's domain error. The distinct type keeps two string-backed concepts from being swapped silently, while equality stays plain equality and the value maps to a column with no wrapping. The type is derived from the declaration rather than written by hand, so the kind is declared once and the type cannot drift from its parser.
- A text value object is declared through the text factory with its bounds, an optional pattern and an optional normalization, and the error to fail with, which must be a domain error and receives the bounds as structured details. Trimming always happens first, so no value object re-implements it.
- `is` is a pure guard: it accepts only a value already in normalized form, so a guard never reports `true` for a value `parse` would have changed.
- A closed set of literals (a role, a status) goes through the literal-set factory, and its type is derived from the set's values, so the type and the runtime list can't drift. A set whose rejection is part of the API contract passes its own error to the factory; otherwise the shared kernel's invalid-literal error is raised.
- A class-based value object with a wrapped field and an `equals` method is not used: it adds an unwrap at every boundary and an equality method the distinct type already gives for free.

## Errors

- Every domain error derives from the shared kernel's base domain error and carries a stable `snake_case` code. Codes are part of the API's stable contract, so the HTTP layer maps a code to a status without knowing each error class, and renaming a class or rewording a message never breaks a client.
- Each sub-slice has one error type, declared by deriving from what the base error's `define` factory returns for a map of code to message. Its code type is derived from that map, so a status map keyed by it fails to compile when a code has no status.
- The message is fixed per code, in English, and never interpolates a value. Variable data (a limit, a current state) travels in the error's structured details, which keeps monitoring grouping one failure as one issue and keeps user-supplied values out of logs.

## Entities and parts

- The state is a named type extending the base entity state, so every entity and part has an `id` the base can compare. Optional state traits from the shared kernel (a version for optimistic concurrency, creation and update timestamps, a soft-delete timestamp) are mixed into that interface only when the table really has those columns, because a trait without a column is a field the mapper has to invent.
- The base assigns the state and freezes the instance, so fields are immutable in the type system and at runtime, as far as the language allows, and the class never repeats its field list in a constructor, a getter or a serializer.
- Construction is restricted (a private constructor, or the language's equivalent) and takes the whole state object, never positional arguments, because two positional arguments of the same type swap silently.
- Creation of the entity goes through intention-named factories that all take `(input, now)`: a named input object plus the clock. Core never generates ids or reads the time, so the caller passes both and a factory stays deterministic. A part is created by an operation of the entity, so the entity stays the only way in.
- Reconstitution goes through the `restore` factory every entity and part inherits from the base. It trusts its input, because the mapper already parsed every field; no entity or part writes its own.
- Identity comparison goes through the inherited `sameAs`, which compares class and id, never through plain equality on instances, because two restores of the same row are different objects that are the same entity.
- No getters and no snapshot method. A field is read directly; a derived fact is a method whose name says it computes (`isActive()`).
- A state-changing operation returns a new instance through the base's protected copy-with-changes patch (`with`), never a mutation of the receiver and never a hand-written copy. A patch never carries the id, so no operation can change an entity's identity. An operation that leaves the state unchanged may return the receiver itself, so a caller can skip the write by identity.

## Events

- An event is a value of the shared kernel's domain event type, with a literal `type` named `<entity>.<past_tense_verb>`, the entity's id, the time passed in by the caller, and a payload of plain values.
- An operation that raises events returns the shared kernel's change result, the new instance as `next` plus its `events`, and never publishes anything itself. The entity can't know whether its change will commit, so only the caller, which owns the transaction, can hand the events to the outbox in the same run.
- An operation that raises no event returns the bare instance, not an empty change result, so the signature says whether events exist.

## Mappers

- The mapper beside a repository has exactly two directions: to the row for writes, and to the domain for reads. To the domain, it parses every column with the same value-object parsers the domain uses and then calls `restore`, so a row that drifted from the domain's rules fails on read instead of flowing in.
- The mapper is the only place that knows both the row type and the state interface. A repository never builds an entity or a row by hand.

---

## Non-goals

- Doesn't decide which entities a context has or where their boundaries are; that belongs to each slice's spec.
- Doesn't govern read models of queries, which are plain views mapped inside each query and never entities.
